#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Round-trip the cloud-sync scripts against the local QA S3 backend.

Drives a ROCKNIX device over SSH - a VM from generic-x64-vm, or real hardware
on the LAN - through backup, restore and content sync, and checks the result
from both ends: file contents on the device, and objects in the bucket.

    ./tools/cloud-test-backend up
    ./tools/cloud-round-trip --host root@127.0.0.1 --port 10022
    ./tools/cloud-round-trip --host root@127.0.0.1 --port 10022 --second-port 10023 \
        --identity /tmp/rocknix-vm-pair/qa-key          # the VM pair: two devices, one cloud

With --second-port the two-device fixtures of #35 run after the single-device
suite (--only A1,A9 runs just those): a conflict is a change on both sides
since two devices last agreed, and only a pair can make one (D-QA-009).

    ./tools/cloud-round-trip --host root@127.0.0.1 --port 10022 --identity ... \
        --path-prefix /tmp/qa-bin --only CAP1,CAP2,CAP3,CAP4,CAP5,CAP6,CAP7,CAP8,CAP9,CAP10,CAP11,CAP12

--only CAP1..CAP12 runs #21's capture fixtures against a staged cloud_capture
on one device with no cloud at all -- no MinIO, no remote, no second guest
(see the capture section below for what to stage). --only A15 runs #86's
identity fixture: one device and the QA endpoint, no second guest. --list
prints every registry without touching a device.

    CLOUD_QA_BWLIMIT=200k ./tools/cloud-test-backend up
    ./tools/cloud-round-trip --host root@127.0.0.1 --port 10022 --identity ... \
        --only LINK1,LINK2,LINK3,LINK4,LINK5,LINK6,LINK7 --serial-socket /tmp/rocknix-qemu-serial.sock

--only LINK1..LINK7 (or --link after the suite) runs #103's link-loss cells:
each starts a cloud operation, cuts the guest's link over its serial console
at a named phase, restores it 40 s later and asserts the run ended within
90 s with an honest exit and nothing partial left behind. They need the
throttled WebDAV endpoint and a guest with a serial console (a headless VM);
on a handheld they are skipped, with a line saying so.

    ./tools/cloud-round-trip --host root@127.0.0.1 --port 10024 --identity ... \
        --only KILL1,KILL2,KILL3,KILL4,KILL5,KILL10,KILL11,KILL12,KILL13,KILL15,KILL16,KILL18 \
        --serial-socket /tmp/rocknix-qemu-serial-c.sock --monitor-socket /tmp/rocknix-qemu-monitor-c.sock

--only KILL1,... (or --kill after the suite) runs #105's KILL cells: each
writer killed at every step it has (a SIGKILL at rclone's per-file line, a
guest-side watcher on a filesystem condition, an exported bash function
that kills from inside the step, a planted torn file), then asserted on
D-CLOUD-078 -- the previous state is what remains, the stamp never says 0,
a re-run completes. KILL12 and KILL13 are power cuts: `system_reset` over
QEMU's monitor with the config files planted torn, or behind
EmulationStation's own page-close writers (the walk in
tools/vm-walks/ui-settings-toggle.steps). Cells needing no endpoint prepare
the device locally; the two power-cut cells need the serial console and the
monitor and are skipped with a line without them. Every fixture in every
registry now ends in litter_scan() -- one last-known-good of each record, no
writer's litter -- and every failure fixture runs its output through
assert_message() -- no exit code, log path or tool name where a player
reads (the three outcome words are asserted by default; --no-vocabulary skips that).

Two guards, both load-bearing:

* Every cloud_* script picks its remote with `rclone listremotes | head -1`.
  If the device has another remote sorting earlier, the tests would sync a
  real cloud account instead. The remote is asserted, never assumed.
* Restore is destructive by design. The suite works in a scratch tree by
  default and only touches /storage/roms with --real-roms, so pointing this
  at a device with real saves cannot quietly delete them.
"""

